WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.490
Most accessibility bugs in components come
from using the wrong element and then

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:07.280
trying to patch the
difference with attributes.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.220
Start with the right element and
much of the work disappears.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:16.780
You will sort links from buttons by
behaviour, set button types inside forms,

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:19.080
and name icon-only controls.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.340
A link goes somewhere. It changes
the URL, opens a new page,

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:29.310
or jumps to a section. Use
an anchor with an href.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:33.620
A button does something. It
submits, opens, toggles,

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.780
deletes, or saves. Use a button.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.260
Styling is a separate question.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.300
A button can look like a link, and
a link can look like a button.

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:46.620
What matters is what happens
when it is activated.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:48.800
This looks fine with a mouse.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:54.500
With a keyboard it cannot be reached by
Tab, it does not respond to Enter or Space,

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:58.000
and a screen reader
announces it as plain text.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:03.360
One element change gives you
focusability, Enter and Space activation,

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:06.940
the button role, and
correct behaviour in forms.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:12.520
Adding role button and tabIndex 0 to the
div would still leave you writing key

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:16.520
handlers by hand, and it is
easy to get subtly wrong.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:20.000
A button inside a form
defaults to type submit.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:25.100
A Cancel or Add another button without
type button will submit the form

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:31.640
unexpectedly. Set the type explicitly on
every button that is not the submit action.

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:37.420
A button that contains only an icon has no
accessible name unless you give it one.

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:42.580
Hide the icon from assistive technology
with aria-hidden so it is not announced

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:45.280
twice, or as a meaningless graphic.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:49.760
Screen reader users often pull up
a list of all links on a page.

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:53.180
A list of five Read more links is useless.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.080
Write link text that
describes the destination,

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:02.580
such as “Read the typography lesson”, or
add visually hidden text that completes it.

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:05.240
A disabled button cannot be focused,

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:10.240
so people using a keyboard may not discover
it or learn why it is unavailable.

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:15.260
When the reason matters, keep the button
enabled and show a clear message on

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:21.620
activation, or use aria-disabled
true with explanatory text nearby.

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:24.700
Links navigate, buttons act.

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:28.040
Pick the element by
behaviour, not appearance.

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:32.140
Set type button on
non-submit buttons in forms.

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:38.200
Give icon-only buttons an accessible name,
and write link text that stands alone.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:44.120
Next, the same idea inside a form: every
field needs a name that stays visible,

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:48.200
and every error needs to be announced
with the field it belongs to.
